{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 2, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond inherited the county at a young age and his early rule was marked by the influence of regents and powerful nobles who controlled the affairs of the county. His minority was a turbulent time as the region faced constant threats from Muslim forces and internal rivalries among Christian leaders. Raymond was eventually declared of age and began to assert his own authority but his reign was plagued by political instability and military defeats.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a close relative of the royal family and often acted as a regent during the minority of King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was appointed regent in 1174 and held the position for several years. During this time he attempted to maintain peace with Muslim neighbors and was known for his cautious diplomacy. He opposed the aggressive policies of Guy of Lusignan and the military orders who favored open warfare. Raymond believed that the survival of the Crusader states depended on alliances and negotiation rather than reckless battles. His stance often put him at odds with other nobles and he was briefly removed from power by factions loyal to Guy.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was involved in the disastrous Battle of Hattin in 1187. He had warned against the campaign led by Guy of Lusignan and had refused to join the army at first. He eventually joined the crusader forces but was captured during the battle along with many other nobles. His capture was a major blow to the Crusader states as he was one of the most experienced and respected leaders. He was imprisoned by Saladin and remained in captivity until his death in 1187. His passing marked the end of a long and complex chapter in the history of the Crusader states. Raymond was remembered as a pragmatic ruler who understood the fragility of Christian holdings in the Levant. His legacy is one of cautious leadership amid overwhelming odds and a deep commitment to preserving the Latin presence in the Holy Land.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.71926071238704e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.03804221749305725}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.008450583554804325}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.014097276143729687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.09045735746622086}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0009839460253715515}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00025376438861712813}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.311301275563892e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.47409361600875854}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0026736250147223473}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.2993727978027891e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"to